.TH EXT2FS.UTIL 8
.SH NAME
ext2fs.util \- Workspace filesystem protocol for ext2
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/filesystems/ext2fs.fs/ext2fs.util
\-p device removable|fixed readonly|writable
.br
.B ext2fs.util
\-P device removable|fixed
.br
.B ext2fs.util
\-m device mountpoint removable|fixed readonly|writable
.br
.B ext2fs.util
\-u device mountpoint
.br
.B ext2fs.util
\-r|\-i device removable|fixed
.SH DESCRIPTION
Device tokens follow the existing helper convention: hd1 addresses
/private/dev/hd1a and its raw counterpart /private/dev/rhd1a.
Probe validates the supported kernel disk profile without changing the volume
and writes ext2fs.name and ext2fs.label in /usr/filesystems/ext2fs.fs.
Mount uses /sbin/mount -t ext2fs and honors read-only access. Writable
mounts of dirty volumes are refused; forced mounting is unsupported.
Repair invokes fsck_ext2fs -p. Initialization invokes newfs_ext2fs with its
supported defaults. Neither operation proceeds when mount inspection fails
or the target is mounted. No automatic repair accompanies a mount.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Uses kernserv/loadable_fs.h FSUR codes. Shell exit status is the code modulo
256: recognized 255, initialization-recognized (-P) 248, unrecognized 254, success 253, I/O failure 252,
unclean 251, invalid arguments 250. Reboot advice is reported after repair.
.SH SEE ALSO
newfs_ext2fs(8), fsck_ext2fs(8), mount_ext2fs(8)
